description:
Send one HTTPS request to an allowed host and return its status and up to 1 MiB of a complete body. Use web_fetch when you need readable page text. Truncated bodies are omitted with an incomplete-source notice. Put a credential placeholder such as ${secrets.NAME.FIELD} in the Authorization header. Supplied headers are removed permanently after an origin change. Temporary URLs stay private. A GET without a body follows up to 20 redirects, admitting every target under the same policy.
Example arguments
{"url":"https://api.example.com/v1/status","method":"GET"}

Read one HTTPS page under the network policy and return text or Markdown. Use it for a page you need to read. Use http_request for an API call or when a credential is required.
Example arguments
{"url":"https://www.iana.org/help/example-domains"}

Send a push notification to the person and return the delivery result. Use it when the person needs an alert outside the conversation. Use message for progress in the conversation or reply directly with the final answer.
Example arguments
{"message":"The requested check is complete.","priority":"normal"}

Find remembered facts by matching their key or content, with key matches first. Only core entries are shown at the start. Use memory_list to find keys and categories when you lack a query.
Example arguments
{"query":"release notes","limit":3}

Return remembered keys and categories, newest first. Only core entries are shown at the start. Use memory_recall when you need the content of a particular fact.
Example arguments
{"category":"note"}

Record a plan the person can see and return its steps and statuses. Use it when several dependent steps or a decision need tracking. For a short answer, reply directly.
Example arguments
{"plan":[{"step":"Read the failing test","status":"in_progress"}]}

Post a progress line to the current conversation and return the delivery outcome. Use it when the person needs an update before your answer. Reply directly for a final answer. Use notify for a separate alert.
Example arguments
{"text":"The build is running."}

Delete a recurring schedule and return the outcome. Use cron_list to find its schedule_id. Use cron_update to pause it while keeping its definition.
Example arguments
{"schedule_id":"review"}

Change a schedule’s expression, zone or message, or pause or resume it, and return the outcome. Use cron_list to find its schedule_id. Use cron_remove to delete it.
Example arguments
{"schedule_id":"review","paused":true}

Create or replace an entire file, creating missing directories, and return the change. Use file_edit to replace selected text in an existing file, or apply_patch for changes across files.
Example arguments
{"path":"notes.md","content":"Ready for review.\n"}

Replace exact text in an existing text file and return the changes. Each old_text must match the original once, and no edits may overlap. All replacements must apply before anything is written. Use file_write to create or replace the whole file, and apply_patch for changes across files.
Example arguments
{"path":"notes.md","edits":[{"old_text":"draft","new_text":"ready"}]}

Start a child agent on a well-scoped task and return an agent_id for wait_agent, send_input, followup_task, close_agent and interrupt_agent. The child can use only your tools or the subset you grant, with the same permissions. It cannot acquire capabilities you do not hold. Use fork_turns none for only the child’s task, a number for that many of your latest turns, or all for your whole history. Answer directly when the work is small. Use wait_agent to collect its answer.
Example arguments
{"message":"Summarise the test failure.","tools":["file_read"],"fork_turns":"none"}
